\section{Conclusion}

A plan--execute--review team costs several times a single reasoning agent, some tasks it solves do not
need it, and it sometimes misses answers a single agent gets right. Deciding per task whether to pay for it is a
constrained decision with a signed loss, solved by a confidence threshold when escalation helps less the
more confident the cheap answer is. \climb{} certifies that family with a variance-adaptive betting test,
and its bound holds whether or not the condition does, given i.i.d.\ data. Uncertified rules of similar cost can lose up to 16 points with no warning at
deployment, while \climb{}'s loss stayed within the tolerance on every setting, spending 62\% of the pipeline's tokens on average
at 5 points and 35\% at 10. Where confidence misleads, it saves little rather than
losing accuracy.
